    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Triumph TF-E Bikes Get Even More Enduro With Updates for the 2027 Model Year\nXpeng Is Offering Self-Driving Rides in Paris, Might Get EU-Wide Certification Ahead of Tesla\n6 photos\nPhoto: Xpeng\nChinese carmaker Xpeng has started offering self-driving rides in Paris using its Tesla FSD rival Navigation Guided Pilot (NGP). The demo rides are available to visitors to Xpeng's showroom through November 28. This marks not only the first time Xpeng has offered NGP rides outside China, but also the first Chinese carmaker demonstrating its self-driving technology in Europe.\nTesla has long bragged about having the most advanced autonomous driving system in the world. Tesla Full Self-Driving has been praised as having \"human-like\" behavior and even \"superhuman\" abilities to sense danger and take evasive maneuvers. Based on FSD, Tesla has also started Cybercab driverless operations in Texas.\nHowever, Elon Musk's company has faced difficulties getting FSD approved in Europe, despite some successes in the Netherlands and a few other markets. The main cause has been Tesla's stubbornness in disregarding UNECE R171 S02 regulations pertaining to advanced driver assistance systems. Instead, Tesla thinks the EU should accept FSD as it was designed in the US by granting it exceptions to the European rules.\nThat explains why Tesla is still struggling to get approval across the block. Mercedes-Benz, on the other hand, has designed its MB.DRIVE Assist Pro system around those pesky regulations. This allowed it to get the first certification in Germany last week. Another carmaker that also observed UNECE restrictions is Xpeng, which appears to be next in line to steal Tesla's limelight.\nXpeng recently announced its European ambitions and has already started self-driving rides in Paris using its Navigation Guided Pilot (NGP). This is an advanced driver assistance system rivaling both Tesla Assisted Driving (FSD's new name in Europe) and Mercedes-Benz's MB.DRIVE Assist Pro. Xpeng is offering visitors to its Paris showroom self-driving demo rides lasting 30 minutes through November 28.\nXpeng touts being the first Chinese brand to demonstrate its self-driving technology in Europe. It's also the first time Xpeng has showcased its tech outside China. The demo car is a production Xpeng L03 Performance Ultra whose brains are powered by two Turing AI chips. Each of them is rated at 750 trillion operations per second (TOPS).\nXpeng's NGP driverless system is a vision-based system for the most part. However, unlike Tesla's, it also uses radar and ultrasonic sensors for additional input. Despite that, it doesn't appear to be as capable as Tesla Assisted Driving. While the system handled Paris traffic with ease during a demo ride, journalists from the French outlet Caradisiac noticed the car did not stop at an amber traffic light and, a few meters later, it also failed to yield for a pedestrian.\nTesla Assisted Driving is not perfect either, which is why both systems are certified as Level 2 driver assistance systems. This means that they must be supervised at all times, and the driver must be ready to take control at any time. Xpeng plans to offer NGP to European customers in 2027, pending regulatory approval.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nxpeng navigation guided pilot xpeng ngp Xpeng driverless rides autonomous driving tesla assisted driving\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Cristian Agatie\nAfter his childhood dream of becoming a \"tractor operator\" didn't pan out, Cristian turned to journalism, first in print and later moving to online media. His top interests are electric vehicles and new energy solutions. Full profile\nUP NEXT Is This the Perfect 1970 Charger Project? Still Unrestored, All-Original, 1 of 436",
